{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1543 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "bfa96194dbeec820e1eea9ddb86d55d1fa7016ea89d7ddc4b541d20733433b3a" :edb/db-before-t 1542 :edb/db-after-t 1543 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534855 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:09:18.834Z" 13194139534855 true] [17592186124130 1100 "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9  + z^2 = 1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186124130 1100 "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} + \\dfrac {y^2} 9 + z^2 = 1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y)$." 13194139534855 false] [17592186124130 1145 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} + z^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nz^{2} &= 1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\\\\nz &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n1 - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\ge 0; \\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\le 1\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {x^2} {4} + \\dfrac {y^2} 9 = 1\n$$\n\nand its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is of $z = z(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\bigg\\{(x, y) \\,:\\, \\dfrac {x^2} {4} + \\dfrac {y^2} 9 \\leq 1 \\bigg\\}\n$$" 13194139534855 false] [17592186124130 1145 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9 =  1$ and its boundary.  \n\nTherefore, the domain is of $z = z(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\bigg\\{ (x, y) \\,:\\, \\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9 \\leq  1 \\bigg\\}.\n$$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186124226 1100 "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {(x-2)^2} {2} +  {y^2} + \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{2} =1,$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139534855 true] [17592186124226 1100 "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {(x-2)^2} {2} + {y^2} + \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{2} =1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139534855 false] [17592186124226 1145 "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - 2)^{2})/(2) + (0)^{2} + ((0 + 1)^{2})/(2) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(2) + \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(2) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx - 2 &= ± 1 \\\\\nx &= 2 ± 1 \\\\\nx &= 1, 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are\n\n$$\n\\left(1, 0, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 0, 0\\right)\n$$" 13194139534855 false] [17592186124226 1145 "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+(0)^{2}+\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $\\left(1, 0, 0\\right)$ and $\\left(3, 0, 0\\right).$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186124230 1100 "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere $(x+1)^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 8z = 20$?" 13194139534855 false] [17592186124230 1100 "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere $(x+1)^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 8z = 20?$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186124230 1145 "The equation of a sphere centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ with radius $r > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-x_0)^2 + (y-y_0)^2 + (z-z_0)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nBy completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} + 8z &= 20 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} + (z^{2} + 8z + 16) &= 20 + 16 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} + (z + 4)^{2} &= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n(x-(-1))^2 + (y-0)^2 + (z-(-4))^2 = 6^2\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point $C(-1,0,-4)$ and the radius $r=6$." 13194139534855 false] [17592186124230 1145 "The equation of a sphere centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ with radius $r > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-x_0)^2 + (y-y_0)^2 + (z-z_0)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nBy completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+8z & =20 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+({z}^{2}+8z+16) & =20+16 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z+4)^{2} & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n(x-(-1))^2 + (y-0)^2 + (z-(-4))^2 = 6^2,\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point $C(-1,0,-4)$ and the radius $r=6.$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186124231 1100 "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the sphere $x^2 + (y+2)^2 + (z-3)^2 = 25$?" 13194139534855 false] [17592186124231 1100 "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the sphere $x^2 + (y+2)^2 + (z-3)^2 = 25?$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186124231 1145 "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} + (0 - 3)^{2} &= 25 \\\\\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} + 9 &= 25 \\\\\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the circle of radius $4$ centered at $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane." 13194139534855 false] [17592186124231 1145 "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}+(0-3)^{2} & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}+9 & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the circle of radius $4$ centered at $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane." 13194139534855 true] [17592186124420 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315141 13194139534855 true] [17592186124420 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315148 13194139534855 true] [17592186124420 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315210 13194139534855 true] [17592186124420 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315231 13194139534855 true] [17592186124424 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315182 13194139534855 true] [17592186124424 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315189 13194139534855 true] [17592186124424 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315196 13194139534855 true] [17592186124425 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315134 13194139534855 true] [17592186124425 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315203 13194139534855 true] [17592186124425 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315217 13194139534855 true] [17592186124425 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315224 13194139534855 true] [17592186124570 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315155 13194139534855 true] [17592186124570 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315162 13194139534855 true] [17592186124570 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315169 13194139534855 true] [17592186124742 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534855 true] [17592186124742 1100 "Given the sphere $x^{2} + (y + 3)^{2} + (z - 1)^{2} = 2$, what is the domain of $z = z(x, y)$?" 13194139534855 false] [17592186124742 1100 "Given the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=2,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186124742 1145 "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (z-1)^{2} & =2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})} \\\\ z & =1\\pm \\sqrt{2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=2\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(0,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane." 13194139534855 true] [17592186124803 1017 #edb/ref 17592186121840 13194139534855 false] [17592186124803 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315176 13194139534855 true] [17592186124803 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534855 true] [17592186124803 1100 "Given the ellipsoid $((x - 1)^{2})/(16) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + (z + 2)^{2} = 1$, find the domain of $z = z(x, y)$." 13194139534855 false] [17592186124803 1100 "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186124803 1145 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (z+2)^{2} & =1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}.\n$$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186125128 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534855 true] [17592186125128 1100 "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere $x^{2} + (y + 3)^{2} + (z - 1)^{2} = 2$?" 13194139534855 false] [17592186125128 1100 "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=2?$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186125128 1145 "Remember that the equation of a sphere centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ with radius $r>0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-{x}_{0})^{2}+(y-{y}_{0})^{2}+(z-{z}_{0})^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can write our equation as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=(\\sqrt{2})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point point $C(0,-3,1)$ and the radius $r=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186125129 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534855 true] [17592186125129 1100 "What is the radius of the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + 6y + (z - 1)^{2} = 16$?" 13194139534855 false] [17592186125129 1100 "What is the radius of the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y+(z-1)^{2}=16?$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186125129 1145 "Remember that the equation of a sphere centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ with radius $r>0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-{x}_{0})^{2}+(y-{y}_{0})^{2}+(z-{z}_{0})^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nBy completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y+(z-1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+6y+9)+(z-1)^{2} & =16+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2}={5}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us a radius of $5.$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186125130 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534855 true] [17592186125130 1100 "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the sphere $(x - 2)^{2} + y^{2} + (z + 2)^{2} = 16$?" 13194139534855 false] [17592186125130 1100 "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the sphere $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z+2)^{2}=16?$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186125130 1145 "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(z+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(z+2)^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186125133 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534855 true] [17592186125133 1100 "Given the ellipsoid $((x + 1)^{2})/(9) + (y + 2)^{2} + ((z - 2)^{2})/(9) = 1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." 13194139534855 false] [17592186125133 1100 "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{9}=1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." 13194139534855 true] [17592186125133 1145 "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{9}+(y+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=-\\frac{8}{3}$ and $y=-\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-\\frac{8}{3},0)$ and $(0,-\\frac{4}{3},0).$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186315134 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315135 13194139534855 true] [17592186315134 1089 "q-164216" 13194139534855 true] [17592186315134 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534855 true] [17592186315134 1100 "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{z}^{2}=1?$" 13194139534855 true] [17592186315134 1113 #uuid "d92bb0fb-ca2e-543b-a67c-2516a62739fc" 13194139534855 true] [17592186315134 1145 "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipsoid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the ellipse with semi-axes $4$ and $1,$ centered at $(3,0)$ in the $xz$-plane." 13194139534855 true] [17592186315135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315136 13194139534855 true] [17592186315135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315137 13194139534855 true] [17592186315135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315138 13194139534855 true] [17592186315135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315139 13194139534855 true] [17592186315135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315140 13194139534855 true] [17592186315135 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534855 true] [17592186315135 1041 "selection" 13194139534855 true] [17592186315135 1104 #edb/ref 17592186315139 13194139534855 true] [17592186315135 1110 #uuid "f600bd91-85b2-5dea-9840-5f1d7258644a" 13194139534855 true] [17592186315136 1034 "An ellipse with semi-axes $4$ and $1,$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane" 13194139534855 true] [17592186315136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534855 true] [17592186315136 1111 #uuid "e40086d5-749b-5222